[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router2
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: router2’s Server Room gateway interface (eth0) IP addressing. The interface is configured as 1.0.0.1/8 instead of the intended /30 subnet.

Mechanism: pc1 is on 1.0.0.5/30 (network 1.0.0.4/30). With router2’s eth0 set to 1.0.0.1/8, the two ends are on mismatched subnets. pc1 treats 1.0.0.1 as off-link (not in 1.0.0.4/30), so it cannot properly establish a default route via that IP and cannot return traffic to 1.0.0.1. Conversely, router2 thinks 1.0.0.5 is directly connected (/8) and ARPs for it, so ARP tables look “REACHABLE” on both sides, but ICMP echo fails because pc1 has no valid route for replies.

Localization and evidence:
- pc1: 1.0.0.5/30 on eth0, no default route; ip neigh shows 1.0.0.1 REACHABLE; ping to 1.0.0.1 fails (100% loss).
- router2: eth0 is 1.0.0.1/8 (ip addr and kernel route show 1.0.0.0/8 via eth0); ping to 1.0.0.5 also fails despite ARP REACHABLE; no firewall drops (iptables ACCEPT), IP forwarding on.

Root cause: During maintenance, router2’s eth0 netmask was changed from /30 to /8, creating a subnet mismatch with the Server Room /30.

Corrective fix: Reconfigure router2 eth0 to 1.0.0.1/30 (network 1.0.0.4/30) and persist it. After correcting, pc1’s default route via 1.0.0.1 will be valid again, and pings will succeed. Also review FRR RIP “network 1.0.0.0/8” to ensure it advertises the correct /30 after the interface fix.
=== END ===